**The controlled-input pattern — a worked example.**

Here is the complete shape you will use any time you need a text input and a form submission:

```jsx
import { useState } from 'react'

function SearchForm() {
  const [text, setText] = useState('')

  function handleSubmit(e) {
    e.preventDefault()       // stop the page from reloading
    console.log('submitted:', text)
  }

  return (
    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>
      <input
        value={text}
        onChange={(e) => setText(e.target.value)}
      />
      <button type="submit">Search</button>
    </form>
  )
}
```

- `value={text}` — React owns the displayed value; it always matches state.
- `onChange={(e) => setText(e.target.value)}` — every keystroke updates state, keeping the two in sync.
- `e.preventDefault()` inside `onSubmit` — without this the browser would POST the form and reload the page, wiping your app state.
